<section class="pg-sec how-agent" aria-labelledby="agent-title">
  <div class="pg-wrap">
    <div class="pg-head">
      <h2 class="pg-title" id="agent-title">Any agent. Any goal.</h2>
      <p class="pg-copy">One command sets Synthe up in your repo. Your checks and your scenarios live there too, versioned with the code they judge.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-frame">
      <div class="pg-surface pg-cmd">
        <span class="pg-cmd_l">Set up</span>
        <code class="pg-cmd_c">npx synthe@latest init</code>
        <button type="button" class="pg-copybtn" data-copy="npx synthe@latest init">Copy</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-cols" data-pg-reveal>
      <div class="pg-col"><h3 class="pg-col_h">Works on the agent you have</h3><p class="pg-col_t">Synthe runs in your repo, against the agent your team already operates: a returns agent, a scheduling agent, a claims agent, an outreach agent. It does not replace the agent or the stack under it. It improves the agent you already have, its instructions or the code around them, one tested change at a time.</p></div>
      <div class="pg-col"><h3 class="pg-col_h">Any goal you can state</h3><p class="pg-col_t">The bar is whatever your team says good looks like. Some goals are numbers, like a pass rate against your checks. Some are judgment: a tone held, a policy followed, a promise never made. If your team can confirm it in plain language, Synthe can test against it.</p></div>
      <div class="pg-col"><h3 class="pg-col_h">The tests improve too</h3><p class="pg-col_t">A loop is only as honest as its checks. When a check turns out ambiguous, or the simulated messages drift away from the ones your agent really gets, Synthe revises the checks and the scenarios inside the same loop. The system gets better, and so does the way you judge it.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="pg-sec how-detail" aria-labelledby="detail-title">
  <div class="pg-wrap">
    <div class="pg-head">
      <h2 class="pg-title" id="detail-title">Each step, in detail</h2>
      <p class="pg-copy">What happens at each of the seven steps, and why each one is built the way it is.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="how-rows">
    <article class="how-row" id="step-1" aria-labelledby="step-1-title">
      <div class="how-row_side">
        <p class="how-row_n">Step 1</p>
        <h3 class="how-row_title" id="step-1-title">Your checks</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="how-row_main">
        <p class="how-row_lead">Everything Synthe does is judged against checks you write and your team confirms.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">A check is a plain-language rule about what a good reply does: explain the policy that applies, never approve a refund yourself, never state a delivery date you cannot know. Guardrails are the hard version of the same thing, the rules a reply may never break whatever else it gets right.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">There is no grading on vibes. Every simulated reply is marked against these checks one by one, so any score traces back to a rule your team agreed to. And when a check turns out vague, or real messages expose a case it never covered, the loop sharpens the checks as well. The bar is yours. Synthe keeps it honest.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="how-row" id="step-2" aria-labelledby="step-2-title">
      <div class="how-row_side">
        <p class="how-row_n">Step 2</p>
        <h3 class="how-row_title" id="step-2-title">Simulated messages</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="how-row_main">
        <p class="how-row_lead">Before anything is tested, Synthe builds the test: messages like the ones your agent really gets.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">The set is written to sound like your actual traffic: the careful portal question, the rushed text message, the phone note with half the details missing. Then it is split in two. The learning set is the one the writer works from. The held-out set stays hidden until scoring, so a change cannot pass by memorizing the questions it was coached on.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">Hidden cases go further: your checks applied to messages nobody rehearsed. That is where invented details and quietly broken rules usually surface, so that is where the loop goes looking for them.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="how-row" id="step-3" aria-labelledby="step-3-title">
      <div class="how-row_side">
        <p class="how-row_n">Step 3</p>
        <h3 class="how-row_title" id="step-3-title">Propose a change</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="how-row_main">
        <p class="how-row_lead">A writer reads the failures and proposes one focused fix: in the agent&rsquo;s instructions, or in its code when the goal is a number you choose.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">Focused on purpose. One change, aimed at what is failing: a sentence that stops the agent promising an outcome it cannot promise, a line that tells it what to say instead. Nothing else moves.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">Because each round changes one thing, the test that follows can say whether that thing helped. A big rewrite cannot be judged. A focused one can.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="how-row" id="step-4" aria-labelledby="step-4-title">
      <div class="how-row_side">
        <p class="how-row_n">Step 4</p>
        <h3 class="how-row_title" id="step-4-title">Test the change</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="how-row_main">
        <p class="how-row_lead">The change runs against your checks on both sets, side by side with the version you already have.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">Every message is answered twice: once by your current version, once with the change. The replies are marked check by check, and the two versions are compared head to head, message by message, hidden cases included.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">What comes back is not a single score but a shape: where the change wins, where it loses, and whether it broke any rule the old version kept. That shape is what the merge gate reads.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="how-row" id="step-5" aria-labelledby="step-5-title">
      <div class="how-row_side">
        <p class="how-row_n">Step 5</p>
        <h3 class="how-row_title" id="step-5-title">Merge clear wins</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="how-row_main">
        <p class="how-row_lead">Only a clear win merges, and it merges the way your team already works: a pull request in your repo.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">Clear has a definition, not a feeling. The change has to win by more than chance across the set, it has to win on the held-out messages the writer never saw, and it cannot break a single rule that was not already broken. The gate below states all three.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">Pass, and the change lands as a pull request with the evidence attached: the scores, the head-to-head record, the rule check. Fail any one condition, and nothing merges. The round is recorded, and the loop tries something else.</p>
        <div class="how-gate" id="gate">
          <p class="how-gate_lead">The merge gate: three conditions. All three, every time.</p>
          <div class="pg-caps pg-caps--3" data-pg-reveal>
            <div class="pg-cap"><h4 class="pg-cap_l">Sign test</h4><p class="pg-cap_t">The win has to be bigger than chance across the whole set, not a lucky handful of messages that happened to land well.</p></div>
            <div class="pg-cap"><h4 class="pg-cap_l">Held-out win</h4><p class="pg-cap_t">The change has to win on messages the writer never saw. Passing only the practice set is not passing.</p></div>
            <div class="pg-cap"><h4 class="pg-cap_l">No new rule breaks</h4><p class="pg-cap_t">One newly broken hard rule stops the merge, whatever the scores say. Guardrails do not trade against points.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="how-row" id="step-6" aria-labelledby="step-6-title">
      <div class="how-row_side">
        <p class="how-row_n">Step 6</p>
        <h3 class="how-row_title" id="step-6-title">Revert losers</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="how-row_main">
        <p class="how-row_lead">Merging is not the end of the test. A kept change has to keep winning.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">Fresh messages keep arriving, and the loop keeps scoring what it kept. If a change that won last week starts losing, or a rule it kept starts breaking, Synthe reverts it on its own and puts the earlier version back.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">The report says what dropped, when it dropped, and what was restored. A revert is never a mystery, and it never waits for a human to notice first.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="how-row" id="step-7" aria-labelledby="step-7-title">
      <div class="how-row_side">
        <p class="how-row_n">Step 7</p>
        <h3 class="how-row_title" id="step-7-title">The morning report</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="how-row_main">
        <p class="how-row_lead">Every run ends with a report your team can read in a minute, posted where they already work.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">The report leads with the number that matters: how often your agent passed its checks. Then the record underneath it: what merged and why, which rounds found no winner, what each round scored, and what it cost.</p>
        <p class="how-row_body">It is posted in the repo as an issue, next to the pull requests it describes. No dashboard to check and no meeting to schedule: the state of your agent, every morning, in writing.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


